{
  "image_name": "2eaa9f26b2909784b1ee015946d3db98.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how phosphatidylinositol 4-kinases and related phosphoinositide signals support cancer-associated behaviors. A central theme is conversion of membrane lipids into signaling intermediates such as PI(4)P, PI(4,5)P2, and PI(3,4,5)P3, which feed into major oncogenic pathways including PI3K–AKT, PLC, KRAS, EGFR, MEK, and ERK. Near the plasma membrane, PI4KIIα appears linked to EFR3, KRAS, and HSP90, helping sustain receptor and RAS signaling that promotes cell proliferation, growth, and survival.\n\nThe middle part emphasizes lysosome-related functions. PI4KIIα is shown supporting autophagosome–lysosome fusion, lysosome stability and degradation, and membrane repair after damage. This repair process appears to involve PI(4)P generation, lipid transfer from the ER, and proteins such as ATG2, fitting into a broader phosphoinositide transfer pathway.\n\nThe bottom section connects Golgi and vesicle trafficking to invasion and immune responses. PI4KIIIβ and PI4KIIα generate PI(4)P at the Golgi, where factors like PITPNC1, GOLPH3, and MYO18A link lipid signaling to vesicle formation and actin-associated transport. These secretory and exocytic vesicles release growth factors and MMP9, supporting invasion, while STING-associated signaling contributes to innate immune activation.",
